from jsonb_to_recordset($json$[{"id":"che-ek-126","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Which set of limiting molar conductivities yields $\\Lambda_m^\\circ$ of NH$_4$OH?","options":["$\\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{NH_4Cl}) + \\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{NaOH}) - \\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{NaCl})$","$\\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{NH_4Cl}) - \\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{NaOH})$","$\\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{NaOH})$ alone","$\\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{HCl}) + \\Lambda_m^\\circ(\\mathrm{NaOH})$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Adding NH$_4$Cl and NaOH supplies NH$_4^+$ and OH$^-$; subtracting NaCl removes the spectator Na$^+$ and Cl$^-$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"kohlrausch","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-127","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"For a weak monobasic acid, $K_a$ is related to the conductivity ratio $\\alpha$ and the molarity $c$ by","options":["$K_a \\approx \\alpha^2 c$","$K_a = \\alpha / c$","$K_a = \\alpha^2 / c$","$K_a = c / \\alpha^2$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Ostwald dilution: $K_a = \\alpha^2 c /(1-\\alpha) \\approx \\alpha^2 c$ when $\\alpha \\ll 1$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"kohlrausch","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-128","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Ethanoic acid at $0.010\\ \\mathrm{M}$ has $\\Lambda_m = 16.0$ and $\\Lambda_m^\\circ = 390\\ \\mathrm{S\\ cm^2\\ mol^{-1}}$. $K_a$ is nearest","options":["$1.7 \\times 10^{-5}$","$4.1 \\times 10^{-2}$","$1.6 \\times 10^{-3}$","$3.9 \\times 10^{-1}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"$\\alpha = 16/390 \\approx 0.041$; then $K_a \\approx \\alpha^2 c = (0.041)^2 \\times 0.010 \\approx 1.7 \\times 10^{-5}$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"kohlrausch","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-129","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A galvanic (voltaic) cell is a device that","options":["converts chemical energy of a spontaneous redox change into electrical energy","uses an external battery to force a non-spontaneous reaction","stores charge on two metal plates like a capacitor","measures conductance of an electrolyte only"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"In a galvanic cell a spontaneous cell reaction drives electrons through an external circuit.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-130","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Inside a working galvanic cell, the cathode is the electrode at which","options":["reduction of the oxidant takes place","the reductant loses electrons","the salt bridge is oxidised","current is carried by neutrons"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"By definition the cathode is the reduction site. In a galvanic cell it is the positive terminal.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-131","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The salt bridge in a galvanic cell is provided mainly to","options":["maintain electrical neutrality in the two half-cells","supply the electrons that travel in the wire","raise the standard electrode potentials","keep the two metals from touching the solution"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Ions from the bridge migrate to cancel charge build-up so the cell can continue to operate.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-132","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The standard hydrogen electrode is assigned, by convention, an $E^\\circ$ of","options":["$0.00\\ \\mathrm{V}$","$+1.00\\ \\mathrm{V}$","$-0.76\\ \\mathrm{V}$","$+0.34\\ \\mathrm{V}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"SHE ($a_{\\mathrm{H}^+} = 1$, $p_{\\mathrm{H_2}} = 1\\ \\mathrm{bar}$) is the reference zero of the $E^\\circ$ scale.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-133","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Standard cell EMF is obtained from standard reduction potentials as","options":["$E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cathode}} - E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{anode}}$","$E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{anode}} - E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cathode}}$","$E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cathode}} \\times E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{anode}}$","$E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cathode}} + 2E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{anode}}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Equivalently $E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{red}}(\\mathrm{oxidant}) + E^\\circ_{\\mathrm{ox}}(\\mathrm{reductant})$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-134","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"In standard cell notation the electrode written on the left is the","options":["anode (oxidation half-cell)","cathode (reduction half-cell)","salt bridge","inert voltmeter terminal"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"The IUPAC line diagram is anode | anodic electrolyte || cathodic electrolyte | cathode.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-ek-135","chapter_id":"chemistry-electrochemistry-and-chemical-kinetics","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The line diagram of the Daniell cell is customarily written","options":["$\\mathrm{Zn}|\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}||\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}|\\mathrm{Cu}$","$\\mathrm{Cu}|\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}||\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}|\\mathrm{Zn}$","$\\mathrm{Zn}|\\mathrm{Cu}||\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}|\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$","$\\mathrm{Pt}|\\mathrm{H_2}||\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}|\\mathrm{Zn}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Zinc is oxidised (left) and Cu$^{2+}$ is reduced on copper (right).","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"cells-emf","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[